## 7. What do you think your most important contributions were?

The most important positive contribution was isolating the Fuentes/Penrose overlap with SAT around the time problem, fixed-background insufficiency, and the need for experimentally reachable quantum-gravity probes.

The most important correction was negative: my first “Fuentes/Penrose preserve GR more than SAT” phrasing was wrong or at least badly framed. Nathan corrected it, and the better formulation emerged:

SAT is not less GR-preserving. SAT preserves GR differently, by reconstructing/importing it into Minkowski geometric grammar, especially through the timesheet.

The most important SAT-specific contribution was the final reframing:

GR and ST are orthogonal; their intersection is SAT; the intersection is so rich that it generates the rest.

That may be a useful clean statement of SAT’s historical/theoretical impetus.

