O(\beta)=C\cap\Sigma_t^{(\epsilon)}(\beta)
$$

where \(O(\beta)\) is the observed 3D morphology, \(\beta=v_{\text{tan}}/c\), and \(\epsilon\) is the effective thickness of the time-slice.

## 7. What do you think your most important contributions were?

The most important positive contribution was helping isolate the UI from ontology.

The second important contribution was catching and preserving the distinction between:

full rotational history
pure separation
tangential velocity
causal/light-cone marker
intersection morphology

The third important contribution was correcting the projection/intersection confusion.

The “circle → oblong → line → two points” sequence is not pure projection if Nathan means dimensional clipping. It requires thickened intersection. In the zero-thickness idealization, a rotated circular object generally clips to two points much more abruptly unless the whole circle remains inside the slice.

The most useful negative result was:

Do not describe the radial line as tangential velocity itself. That conflates vector meaning. Better:

The radial line is the one-unit normal causal marker in the nonrotating case. Rotation/tangential speed tilts or reorients the effective causal-slice relation, which changes how the circular particle-worldline intersects the time-slice.

## 8. Was anything actually demonstrated, verified, independently checked, numerically tested, symbolically checked, simulated, or reproduced?

No numerical simulation, external verification, or formal derivation occurred.

What was checked was internal geometric consistency at the toy-model level.

Specific geometry established:

For a rotating equatorial point on an ordinary sphere of radius \(R_\star\):

$$
p(t)=R_\star(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t,0)
$$

Pure separation from the starting point:

$$
\Delta p(t)=R_\star(\cos\omega t-1,\sin\omega t,0)
$$

Magnitude:

$$
|\Delta p(t)|=2R_\star\left|\sin\frac{\omega t}{2}\right|
$$

For a nonrotating baseline with an outer sphere one unit larger:

$$
p_0=(R_\star,0,0)
$$

$$
q_0=(R_\star+1,0,0)
$$

$$
q_0-p_0=(1,0,0)
$$

So the connector is one unit and surface-normal.

Those are basic geometric checks, not physical validation.

## 9. What did Nathan explicitly correct, sharpen, reject, or insist on?

Nathan corrected several things:

1. “UI is not the theory.”

He explicitly stated that the UI is an analytical tool for understanding and perhaps calculating things inside the assumptions of the theory, not ontology.

2. “Unit hypersphere” does not mean physically fixed radius.

Both hyperspheres remain unit hyperspheres in their own grids. The transformable grid changes relation to the fixed grid under expansion/rotation.

3. In practice, the stationary grid dominates.

The main analysis often concerns the geometry of a line relative to one or both hyperspheres at a given time.

4. The neutron-star toy model is two spheres, not two hyperspheres.

This was a visualization simplification before later bumping the calculation back up to four dimensions.

5. Ordinary physical neutron-star rotation remains ordinary 3D rotation.

When moving to the real model, the coordinate geometry is higher-dimensional, but the star itself should not be treated as undergoing arbitrary \(SO(4)\) spin.

6. “Separation” was not the right word for the full object.

The full object is the entire rotational history. Pure separation is only one diagnostic.

7. Dimensional clipping means intersection, not projection.

This is central.

8. Finite slice thickness must be included.

Without slice thickness, the desired circle/oblong/line/two-points behavior collapses into a more abrupt ideal intersection case.

